        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'A WhatsApp message containing a clear divorce declaration can constitute a valid talaq pronouncement in UAE. The UAE Supreme Court has upheld digital communications as legal acts. However, the talaq has no legal effect until the husband registers it at the Personal Status Court within 15 days under Article 58 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024. An unregistered WhatsApp divorce leaves your marriage legally intact and your financial rights unaffected.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Is a text message divorce valid in UAE?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Yes, an SMS or text message containing a clear divorce declaration can be a valid talaq pronouncement under UAE law, based on Federal Law No. 5/1985 (Civil Code) and Federal Law No. 1/2006 (E-Transactions Law). The message must express clear intent to divorce. However, it must still be registered at the Personal Status Court within 15 days to have legal effect. Without registration, the marriage continues legally.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'What if my husband sent a divorce message but denies it?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'If your husband sent a WhatsApp or SMS divorce message but denies it or claims it was a joke, you can seek judicial registration of the talaq by filing at the Personal Status Court. UAE courts will examine the message content, metadata, and context. Authenticated screenshots, read receipts, and message timestamps are admissible. WhatsApp messages are difficult to credibly deny once the court orders a technical extraction from the device.',
